## Memory (FALDA)

This project has a FALDA memory store connected via MCP (`falda_*` tools).

- Before starting non-trivial work, call `falda_recall` with a natural-language
  query. It assembles the best available context across all memory tiers
  (facts, preferences, rules, past episodes, project core) in one call — you
  do not need to decide which tier to query.
- A small amount of memory context may already be injected automatically at
  the start of a session, wrapped in a `<falda-auto-recall>` block — this is
  a smaller-budget, best-effort recall fired before you asked for anything,
  not a substitute for calling `falda_recall` yourself. Call `falda_recall`
  whenever you need more than what's already there.
- When you learn something durable worth remembering long-term (a fact,
  preference, constraint, rule, or standing instruction — not a transient
  detail), call `falda_remember` with `{ content, type }`. The field is
  `content`, not `text`. Each call records a new memory — to correct a
  previously remembered fact, call `falda_remember` again with the corrected
  content rather than expecting the old one to be edited.
- To stop recalling a memory that's outdated or wrong, call `falda_forget`
  with its `atom_id` (from a `falda_remember` result or a `falda_recall`
  hit). This only stops it from surfacing — it is not privacy erasure.
- Before a context compaction/summarization, or at the end of a substantial
  work session, call `falda_distill` so this session's raw turns get
  distilled into durable memories before the working context is truncated.
  It's asynchronous (returns a `job_id`; poll with `falda_distill_status` if
  you need to confirm completion). A background worker also runs
  periodically, so this is a safeguard against losing a session's learnings
  to compaction, not something to call on every turn.
- Raw conversation turns are captured automatically; you do not need to call
  `falda_stream_add` yourself except to backfill or annotate.
- Scenes and core are not directly editable and are not separate tools here
  — they're maintained by FALDA's distillation pipeline and surfaced to you
  through `falda_recall`.
- If recall results seem off (e.g. missing what you just wrote), call
  `falda_whoami` to confirm which tenant this connection resolves to.
